WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Pull up a chair, this is Models in Agents, Episode 59,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.140
from May 25th, 2026.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.100
Your daily AI briefing.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.400
Monday in the AI world,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.420
which means a weekend's worth of announcements

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.360
to catch up on.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:13.220
Let's go.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.480
Data sets new slash key jump menu

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.280
now launches agent conversations

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.100
directly from your databases.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.400
Simon Willisons shipped data set 1.0 alpha 30

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.820
with a keyboard driven jump to menu

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.160
that plugins can extend.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.840
The data set agent plugin adds a conversation starter

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.580
form directly into that menu.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.300
New Extract 3, a new 4 billion parameter vision language

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.360
model arrived on hugging phase for structured extraction

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.560
and markdown conversion from documents.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.580
A custom inference engine now runs mini CPM V4.6

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:55.580
on the Ascend 310B NPU inside the $149 orange pi AI

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.480
Pro board.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.920
These small targeted releases lower the friction

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.560
for mixing data exploration with agent workflows

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.260
this week.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.560
The top story this week is the data set release

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.840
that turns a data exploration tool into a lightweight

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.560
launch pad for agent sessions.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.020
Data set 1.0 alpha 30 introduces a jump to menu

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.380
triggered by the slash keyboard shortcut.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.740
Users can type to reach databases, tables, or canned queries

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.180
without leaving the interface.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.760
The release includes plugin hooks so extensions can

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.220
inject additional content into the menu and its empty state.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.360
The data set Iphone agent plugin uses this hook to surface

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.960
a form that starts a new agent conversation.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.640
A live demo is available at agent.dataset.io

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.140
after GitHub's sign-in.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.920
More implementation details appear on the data set blog.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.500
Teams already running data set can upgrade to 1.0 alpha 30

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.540
and test the plugin immediately.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.640
Now for the model updates.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.460
New Extract 3 is a 4 billion parameter vision language

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.160
model released on hugging face.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.180
It handles both structured JSON extraction

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.140
from text or images and high quality image

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.380
to mark down conversion.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.020
The model accepts text, images, or mixed inputs

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.200
and supports multilingual documents

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.260
plus separate reasoning and non-reasoning modes.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.920
GGUF, NVFP4, MLX, and VLLM quantized versions

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.300
are already available.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.160
Builders working on document pipelines, OCR or RAG

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.300
preprocessing should test it on receipts, invoices,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.540
and tables this week.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:43.200
Next, twin 3.6, 27B, and 35BBF, 16 models

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.440
were benchmarked on a two times RTX Pro 6,000 setup.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.620
Using the latest stable VLLM backend.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.240
At 64 concurrency, the 27B model reached 1,600 tokens

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.700
per second generation without MTP

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.860
and 1,800 tokens per second with MTP2 enabled.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.540
The 35B model hit 2,700 tokens per second

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.240
at 64 concurrency and 3,500 tokens per second

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.100
at 128 concurrency.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.540
It also delivered 30,000 tokens per second prompt processing.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.360
These numbers come from a personal project

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.480
and give concrete throughput expectations

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.320
for mid-sized twin variants on dual professional GPUs.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.860
A custom C++ inference engine was written

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.200
from scratch to run many CPM V4.6 natively

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:43.880
on the Ascend 310 BNPU inside the $149 orange Pi AI Pro board.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.240
The engine bypasses Torch underscore NPU

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.900
entirely on the hot path.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.000
It uses custom Ascend C kernels for cube mat mall,

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.580
L meters underscore head chunking

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:00.680
and vectorize causal cun 1D to reach 5.90 tokens

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.180
per second FP16 after incremental optimizations.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.620
Python is used only for cold path tokenization

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.140
and image preprocessing.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.700
Anyone targeting edge VLM deployment on this hardware now

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.480
has an open source starting point at the link to GitHub repo.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.880
Mining lamp AI released citer, a small SDK

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:26.560
that adds W888 activation quantization

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.240
on top of MLX for Apple Silicon.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.320
On an M5 Pro, the change reduced pre-fill time

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:39.540
for a 4B VLM from 2.839 seconds to 2.519 seconds

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.660
at 4,516 token context.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.520
Decode speed state essentially flat.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.540
Per channel INT8, tensor ops, require M5 or newer

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.180
while M4 falls back to the regular path.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.960
The repo includes accuracy numbers on WCA text 2 for twin 38B

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.280
and LAMA 38B showing modest perplexity impact.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.200
Now turning to agent and tool developments.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.500
A developer released AI sandbox hyphen manager,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.840
an LXC-based VM template system

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.160
that gives agents full pseudo access.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.300
GPU pass through and browser automation

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.220
while isolating them from the host.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.600
The setup supports multiple concurrent sessions,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.580
persistent.env files and hooks to block dangerous Git operations.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.780
It was built and tested on DGX Spark hardware

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.660
where standard Docker GPU pass through is difficult.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.280
The repo includes CUA for computer use support on Linux.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.660
A new repo called MCP from scratch

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.940
walks through building a model context protocol server

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.620
and client in plain node.js.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.520
It then adds local GGUF inference via node, LAMA, CPP

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.300
and a custom plan act observe agent loop.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.700
The later modules demonstrate MCP sampling

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.680
and tool use with a local model

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.080
plus an optional lane chain path.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.780
The project is intentionally minimal

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.180
and aimed at developers who want to understand

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.660
the protocol mechanics rather than use a high level SDK.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.760
In the practical and community section,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.040
a pull request to GGML hyphen org slash LAMA.CPP

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.840
improves checkpoint creation.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.140
Agentic coding sessions no longer force

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.680
full prompt reprocessing after context

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.680
rewriting tools modify history.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.320
The change targets the common pattern

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.580
where an agent produces 20k plus tokens of code

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.180
and the next short user message triggers

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.640
expensive reprocessing of 70k token contexts.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:51.420
Early users report noticeably more responsive

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.300
agentic coding loops after adopting the patch.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:58.160
Okay, let's pop the hood on activation quantization.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.640
Everyone talks about activation quantization

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:04.860
as if it is a simple turn on INT8 switch

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.120
that always delivers free speed.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.000
In practice, it requires custom kernels

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.000
that only compile on newer silicon

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.620
and trades small accuracy losses for pre-fill gains

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.860
that appear mainly at longer contexts.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.740
The citer implementation registers metal primitives

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:27.060
so the per channel W888 path runs 1.84 times faster

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.800
than W8A16 at M equals 4,096.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.320
Yet decode speed barely moves

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.660
because memory bandwidth remains the limiter.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:41.240
On M4 hardware, the same code silently falls back,

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.060
showing that the technique is gated

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:47.260
by hardware tensor op support rather than software availability.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.300
Perplexity impact stays under 0.2 points

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.360
on 8B models when using per channel scaling.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:57.180
Per group scaling tightens the bound further

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.040
when precision matters more than the 300 millisecond

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:00.920
pre-fill saving.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.160
The gacha that bites most teams is assuming

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.400
the speed up will appear on every workload.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.920
Short context chat sees almost none of the benefit

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.780
while long document R-A-G pre-processing sees the full win.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.860
Use it when your pipeline is pre-fill heavy

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.880
and you control the deployment hardware.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.180
Otherwise, stick with weight only quantization

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.380
until the next silicon generation lands.

